# IPRU NexGen — Phase 1 Addendum: Inline Selection Chip

**Reference:** `IPRU_NexGen_Project_Spec.md` and `IPRU_NexGen_Phase1_Build_Prompt.md`. This addendum refines §5 (Commit behavior) of the Phase 1 prompt — implement this instead of the plain-text commit described there for **tools**, and use the same chip pattern for the **assist** trigger token itself (separate from the AssistBanner, see §3 below).

---

## 1. Problem with current spec

Right now, once a tool is committed (e.g. `@chat-with-doc`), the raw trigger text `@chat-with-doc` just sits in the input as plain characters. This should instead render as a small inline chip.

---

## 2. Chip behavior (tools)

When a tool is committed (via Enter, click, or Space-after-exact-match):

- Replace the raw `@chat-with-doc` text with an inline, non-editable **chip** rendered inside the input, showing:
  - The tool's icon (from `toolsConfig.icon`)
  - Its display label in title case (e.g. "Chat With Doc" — derive from `toolsConfig.label`, not the raw trigger string)
  - A small `×` at the end of the chip
- The chip is a single atomic unit in the input:
  - Clicking the `×` removes the entire chip in one action, same as if it was never typed, cursor lands where the chip was.
  - Pressing `Backspace` with the cursor immediately after the chip removes the **entire chip** in one keystroke (not one character at a time) and restores focus to plain text input at that position.
  - The chip cannot be partially edited — no clicking into the middle of it, no arrow-key entry into it. Cursor navigation (`←`/`→`) skips over it as one unit.
- Text can be typed normally before and after the chip in the same message (chips are inline, not block-level), matching the existing "tools can be referenced anywhere in the prompt" behavior.
- Multiple tool chips in a single message are allowed (e.g. "`@chat-with-doc` then `@create-doc`" both committed) — each behaves independently per the above.

---

## 3. Chip behavior (assists)

Assists still get the `AssistBanner` docked to the composer (per Phase 1 §5) for the duration of the session — that does not change. In addition:

- At the point in the text where the user typed the `/assist` trigger, also replace it with the same chip style (icon + label + `×`) rather than leaving raw `/pst-assist` text or fully deleting it silently.
- Clicking that chip's `×`, or backspacing it away, should both remove the chip **and** end the assist session (i.e. also dismiss the `AssistBanner` and clear the selected-assist state). This becomes the canonical "exit assist mode" affordance — resolving the open question from Phase 1 §5/§7: exiting assist mode happens by removing its chip (via `×` or backspace), not via a separate banner close button. If you want a redundant `×` on the banner itself too, it should trigger the same removal logic as removing the chip.

---

## 4. Implementation notes

- Use each config entry's `icon` field (already present in `assistConfig.ts` / `toolsConfig.ts` from Phase 1) — no new icons need sourcing unless a tool/assist is missing one, in which case flag it rather than guessing a placeholder.
- Chip should be visually distinct from plain text (background pill, rounded corners) but stay compact enough not to disrupt normal typing flow.
- This is a pure frontend/input-model change — no backend involvement. What ultimately gets sent to the backend (Phase 2+) should serialize the chip back into its canonical trigger form (`@chat-with-doc`, `/pst-assist`) for the API payload, even though the user sees the friendly chip — i.e. the chip is a display/interaction layer over the same underlying token, not a different data model.

---

## 5. Acceptance checklist

- [ ] Committing a tool renders an icon + label + `×` chip instead of raw `@trigger-text`.
- [ ] Committing an assist renders the same chip style at the trigger's position, in addition to the existing `AssistBanner`.
- [ ] Chip `×` removes the whole chip in one action.
- [ ] Backspace immediately after a chip removes the whole chip in one keystroke, not character-by-character.
- [ ] Cursor arrow keys treat the chip as a single unit (skip over, not into).
- [ ] Removing an assist's chip (via `×` or backspace) also ends the assist session and dismisses the banner.
- [ ] Multiple chips can coexist in one message, each independently removable.
- [ ] On send, chips serialize back to their canonical `@trigger` / `/trigger` string for the backend payload.
